        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Size a riding mower from the grass you actually mow, then let the narrowest gate, the tightest gap between obstacles and the steepest slope cut that number down. For most suburban acre-to-two-acre lawns that lands on 42 inches, and the wider decks earn their price only on open ground where they can run straight.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pick the machine type by slope, the engine by margin rather than headline horsepower, and confirm overall width with the chute down before you buy. Get those right and the deck you choose will save time every week instead of costing you trimming, scalped crests or a mower that lives outside.
          </p>
